From: Stained Glass OS <ke7oxh@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] winspool, gdi32, winprint, spoolss: printer makers' own Windows drivers install and print as on Windows

Most printer makers ship a whole driver of their own (Brother, Epson,
Zebra, Samsung, Kyocera, Lexmark...): a DLL that draws the page itself.
Of a corpus of real packages from the makers and the Microsoft Update
Catalog, few installed and none of these printed:

- Installing a package (InstallPrinterDriverFromPackage) missed what makers
  write in their INFs: the driver's files named in a DataSection (Brother),
  files installed under another name ("destination,source": Epson), files
  compressed for setup (name.dl_), files in the source disk's cabinet
  (SourceDisksNames' tag file: HP), files for other directories
  (DestinationDirs 66001/66002/66003: print processors, language monitors,
  colour profiles), the package's print processor and language monitor
  (now added with AddPrintProcessor and AddMonitor), core sections of the
  same INF (Canon). A model list not decorated for 64-bit Windows (an
  x86-only package) is no longer installed on 64-bit, as on Windows.
- The core drivers' files are ours: a package carrying its own copies of
  them (some makers ship the system's) gets ours.
- The drivers write their output with WritePrinter on the printer handle
  they are given, not EngWritePrinter: while the print processor runs the
  driver, that handle's writes are the job's data for the port (or the
  file of our 64-bit host).
- Drivers that draw on a surface of their own (EngCreateDeviceSurface) or
  hook the blits on their bitmap get the page as GDI gives it them: a
  picture through their DrvCopyBits (or DrvBitBlt), with a colour
  translation object (XLATEOBJ) to their palette. The engine's blits
  (EngCopyBits, EngBitBlt, EngStretchBlt[ROP], EngTransparentBlt,
  EngEraseSurface) now convert between all the DDI formats (1-32 bits,
  indexed or not), with the raster operations, stretching and an ordered
  dither for palettes of few colours; HT_Get8BPPFormatPalette gives a
  halftone palette.
- Engine services they import that were missing or stubs: EngLoadModule,
  EngLoadModuleForWrite, EngMapModule, EngFindResource, EngFreeModule,
  EngGetPrinterDataFileName, EngGetDriverName (Brother reads its data file
  so), EngQueryLocalTime, EngGetCurrentCodePage, Eng(MultiByte|Unicode)
  conversions, Eng*Semaphore; ColorMatchToTarget.
- winspool: a configuration DLL is given a handle to the printer when the
  program gave only its name (Zebra's read the printer through it and
  failed, leaving a 999 x 39 mil page); DeletePrinterData(Ex), Delete-
  PrinterKey, DevQueryPrint, PrinterMessageBox and SetPort (a language
  monitor's port status) exist.
- spoolss: the router calls makers' print processors and language
  monitors import (OpenPrinter, ReadPrinter, WritePrinter, GetJob,
  SetJob, ...) lead to winspool; GetJobAttributes and the
  bidirectional-request allocators exist.

Zebra's ZDesigner and Brother's HL-L2300 drivers now print (ZPL and
Brother PCL), 64-bit and 32-bit programs.

Test: test/drvpkg-gate.sh with test/drvpkg-umpd.c and test/drvpkg-pack.py
(mutants SG_MUTANT_DATASECTION, RENAME, EXPAND, CABINET, DESTDIRS,
DECORATION, COREFILES, DRVWRITE, UIHANDLE, DRVCOPYBITS, ENGMODULE,
ENGBLT); test/printdrv-gate.sh still passes.
---
--- /dev/null
+++ b/dlls/gdi32/engblt.c
@@ -0,0 +1,704 @@
